    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Expressions-of-Interest--Indigenous-Employment-Opportunities-at-the-University-of-Melbourne_JR-006839": {
      "title": "Expressions of Interest: Indigenous Employment Opportunities at the University of Melbourne",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for current and future opportunities across both professional and academic positions. Whether you are just starting your career or seeking the next step in your professional or academic journey, we encourage you to express your interest in joining our vibrant and diverse community. You may express interest in the following professional areas: Finance | Call Centre & Customer Service | Student Support Services | HR, Recruitment & Culture | Legal | Fundraising | Administration & Office Support | Management Roles | Strategy, Culture & Analytics | Information & Communications Technology | Marketing & Communications You may express interest in the following academic roles across all faculties: Tutor | Research Assistant | Research Fellow | Lecturer | Associate Professor Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfillment, including:  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance, including Cultural Leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program with access to Indigenous counsellors for Indigenous staff Dedicated and ongoing support from Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team A vibrant and active Indigenous Staff Network, providing opportunities for connection and professional development For more information check out our benefits page!  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. We believe the diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing reasonable adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. For assistance, please contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Contact details for other enquiries are located at the bottom of this page. Find more information for applicants requiring reasonable adjustments. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au for further information and support.  Our Indigenous strategy, Murmuk Djerring outlines our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff and students.   Please note : This role requires current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available.   A Working with Children Check (WWCC) is required for all positions. If successful, we'll guide you through the WWCC application process during onboarding and reimburse the cost. Join Us and unlock your career potential!   If this role is right for you, please apply with the following documents:  Resume   Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience Proof of Aboriginality Please note: As part of the application, you’ll be asked to share your preferences for the types of roles and levels of experience that best match your background. For recruitment queries, email Olivia korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Include the Position Number and Job Title in the subject line. Please do not send applications to this email address."
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Allan-Myers-Chair-in-Equity-and-Trusts_JR-016000": {
      "title": "Allan Myers Chair in Equity and Trusts",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-30\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time (1.0 FTE), Continuing Faculty: Faculty of Law Location:  Parkville, Melbourne Salary: Level E $236,824 p.a. plus 17% superannuation  Prestigious inaugural chair at Australia’s top-ranked law school / Shape global understanding of Equity and Trusts / Lead and mentor exceptional scholars. Join Melbourne Law School as the Allan Myers Chair and advance Equity and Trusts scholarship worldwide. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.    The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   In this prestigious chair, you will lead world-class scholarship in Equity and Trusts at Melbourne Law School. You will shape curriculum, mentor researchers, and strengthen global collaborations across leading law schools and institutions. You will influence contemporary private law debates and enhance Melbourne Law School’s international reputation.   What you will deliver:   Lead pioneering research in Equity and Trusts, producing high-impact publications and securing competitive research funding. Design and deliver outstanding teaching in JD and postgraduate programmes, innovating curriculum in Equity and Trusts. Supervise and mentor PhD and higher degree students, nurturing the next generation of private law scholars. Provide collegial leadership within Melbourne Law School, fostering collaboration across disciplines, centres and international partners. Engage widely with profession, government and community, enhancing public understanding of Equity and Trusts. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a good first degree in law and a doctoral qualification in a relevant legal field. Bring significant expertise in Equity and Trusts, including deep knowledge of doctrines, institutions and contemporary developments. Demonstrate an outstanding international reputation through a substantial publication record and collaborative research leadership. Offer extensive experience in graduate-level teaching, curriculum design and student advising within tertiary legal education. Provide evidence of mentoring PhD candidates and early career academics, supporting their research impact and career progression. Exhibit strong leadership, organisational and interpersonal skills, with capacity to shape strategic direction in private law. For further information please refer to the attached Candidate Information Pack.   What we offer you We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page    About Us - Melbourne Law School   Melbourne Law School is Australia’s top-ranked law school and a global leader in legal education and research. You will join a vibrant community of award-winning teachers, leading scholars, dedicated mentors and an engaged alumni network working across more than 20 research centres and institutes. Our strong international partnerships, including with Oxford, Cambridge, NYU and NUS, create exceptional opportunities for collaboration and impact. The team values excellence, innovation, ethical practice and diverse perspectives, supporting you to shape private law scholarship locally and globally.   Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description.   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Michelle Foster at m.foster@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Emily Haagensen at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Emily Haagensen on +61 428 005 853 or at snr-talentacq@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: Wednesday 23rd September 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:   JR-016000 Allan Myers Chair in Equity and Trusts - Candidate Information Pack.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Early-Childhood-Educator-s---Teacher-s--Casual-Positions_JR-016427": {
      "title": "Early Childhood Educators & Teachers, Casual Positions",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\n\nPlease apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE Role type: Casual positions Department : Student and Scholarly services Support high-quality early learning / collaborate in inclusive, research-informed practice / join a respected University childcare service Apply now to contribute your expertise in early childhood education across our vibrant University children’s centres. Investing in you – health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity You will support high-quality early childhood programs across our University centres, grounded in evidence-based pedagogy. You will collaborate with experienced colleagues to create inclusive, play-based learning that strengthens every child’s wellbeing. You will gain varied experience supporting different age groups, rooms, and services within a child-safe organisation.   What you will deliver: Plan, implement, and evaluate play-based learning programs aligned with EYLF, VEYLDF, and service philosophies. Document children’s learning, development, and wellbeing to inform individual planning and continuous programme improvement. Maintain safe, inclusive environments through active supervision, risk management, and adherence to all regulatory requirements. Build strong, respectful relationships with children, families, and colleagues to support belonging and collaborative practice. Collaborate with room leaders and teams to reflect on practice and support service quality improvement.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold an approved Early Childhood degree and current WWCC or VIT registration. Demonstrate sound knowledge of NQF, National Law and Regulations, and Victorian Child Safe Standards. Bring proven experience delivering high-quality education and care using EYLF, VEYLDF, and evidence-based pedagogies. Create inclusive, stimulating environments that promote children’s agency, participation, and strong sense of belonging. Apply diligent risk management, active supervision, and child-safe practices in dynamic early childhood settings. Adapt flexibly across rooms, age groups, and teams while maintaining professional, collaborative conduct.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services supports students and staff from first enquiry through to graduation and beyond. Early Childhood Education Services operates three high-quality University children’s centres, delivering evidence-based programmes for infants to preschoolers. You will join a collaborative, research-informed team committed to child safety, inclusion, and continuous quality improvement.   Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants  We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials: Please ensure to apply through this link We will NOT be accepting applications through the UOM Careers Page You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience to the link provided as part of the application process. Please apply via this link: https://careers.giighire.com/universityofmelbourne/29027?source=link DO NOT APPLY VIA THE UOM CAREERS PAGE   Position Description: Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.docx Early Childhood Educator’s & Teacher’s - Casual Positions.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Lecturer--Indigenous-Social-Equity-and-Leadership---Expression-of-Interest_JR-014395": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership - Expression of Interest",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe University of Melbourne is inviting Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander candidates to submit an Expression of Interest (EOI) for a lecturer, Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership position on a full-time continuing basis. This opportunity is offered at Academic Level B, with a salary range of $124,656–$148,023 per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).   Your next career opportunity:   You will design, teach and assess postgraduate subjects in Social Change Leadership for diverse experienced Fellows. You will advance research on Indigenous social change while supporting Fellows’ community-led projects and long-term leadership journeys. You will collaborate across the University and global Atlantic Fellows network to grow your academic and impact profile.  What you will deliver:   Design, deliver and coordinate engaging postgraduate subjects, using culturally grounded, flexible teaching approaches for adult learners.  Supervise and support Fellows to develop rigorous social change project proposals with strong community partnerships and ethical foundations.  Conduct and publish high-quality research on Indigenous social change and leadership, securing external funding where appropriate.  Contribute to AFSE’s research, seminars and public engagement, representing AFSE locally, nationally and internationally.  Build and sustain collaborations with Indigenous communities, partners and University colleagues to strengthen AFSE’s teaching and impact.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a doctoral qualification, or be near completion, in a field related to Indigenous social change.  Demonstrate experience co-designing curriculum that embeds Indigenous knowledges, cultural safety principles and decolonial teaching practices.  Show a strong record, or clear potential, in research, publication and collaborative project development.  Exhibit excellence in teaching adult learners, including experienced leaders, using inclusive and reflexive pedagogical approaches.  Work effectively within small, interdisciplinary teams, contributing constructively to programme development and shared strategic priorities.  Engage confidently with Indigenous communities and partners, nurturing ethical, long-term relationships that support social change.  Your new team:   Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity You will join the Atlantic Fellows for Social Equity, a collaborative programme advancing Indigenous and Pacific-led social change. AFSE combines a rich Foundation year of fellowship and postgraduate study while nurturing a lifelong community of senior Fellows. The team works closely with communities, partners and a global Atlantic Fellows network to amplify impact. You will collaborate with committed colleagues across the University, including the Faculty of Education, benefiting from strong mentoring and development opportunities.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.  Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.  Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our  Indigenous staff page  to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at  oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit  \"apply with us\"  to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application. Want to know more?   For queries related to this expression of interest, please contact Program Director, Dr. Yvonne Ualesi at yvonne.ualesi@unimelb.edu.au      Position Description: PD_JR-014395 EOI Lecturer Indigenous Social Equity and Leadership.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Venture-Partner--MEC_JR-017234": {
      "title": "Venture Partner, MEC",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nMEC Venture Partner Join the Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre’s Venture Studio in a two-year, full-time Senior Manager 1 role and help turn University research and emerging technologies into investable companies. Help build the next generation of companies based on world-class University research. Work at the intersection of research, entrepreneurship, investment and industry. Shape a new model for university venture creation.   Investing in you: 17% superannuation, salary packaging, flexible work arrangements, wellbeing support, discounted services and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your new team – Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre (MEC) The Melbourne Entrepreneurial Centre advances the University of Melbourne’s vision for innovation and entrepreneurship, helping transform research and ideas into ventures with lasting social and economic impact. MEC brings together researchers, founders and commercialisation expertise across the University’s innovation ecosystem.   Your next career opportunity As a Venture Partner, you will provide hands-on commercial leadership across a portfolio of emerging ventures—assessing opportunities, validating markets, shaping commercial strategy, building teams and maintaining momentum. You will bring together researchers, founders, investors and industry partners, help evolve the Venture Studio’s company-building model and, where appropriate, serve as a director of portfolio companies.   What you will deliver: Assess emerging research and technologies to identify opportunities with venture-scale potential and determine whether they are suited to the Venture Studio model. Help build and progress ventures through customer discovery, market validation, commercial strategy, business model development and evidence generation. Provide commercial leadership across multiple ventures, identifying the next critical steps, sequencing activities effectively and maintaining momentum through different stages of company development. Identify, attract and support commercial founders, executives, advisers and specialist contributors required to build successful companies. Build trusted relationships with researchers and commercial cofounders, that enable you to work closely together to shape opportunities and build successful companies. Act as a director on Venture Studio portfolio companies, contributing to governance, strategic direction and company stewardship. Contribute to the ongoing development of the Venture Studio's company-building practices, methods and operating model.   You may be a great fit if: You have experience building companies or leading commercial growth, venture development or execution in startups, new ventures or other innovation-led environments. You are commercially curious and can identify and shape commercial opportunities around complex research or emerging technologies. You bring strong commercial judgement across customer discovery, market validation, commercial strategy, team formation and investment readiness, and can prioritise the next steps that will create momentum. You can work independently in ambiguity, make sound decisions with incomplete information and use evidence and rapid learning to reduce uncertainty. You are proactive and relationship-focused, with the confidence to build trusted networks, navigate complex stakeholders and bring the right people together. You bring sound judgement, professionalism and experience contributing to governance, strategic oversight or company stewardship.   Experience in one or more of the following would be highly regarded: venture building or venture studios; startups, scale-ups, entrepreneurship or company building, particularly in deep or emerging technologies; investment, venture capital or fundraising; sales and revenue growth; commercial partnerships and deal-making; innovation and commercial strategy execution.   A PhD or equivalent experience that demonstrates an ability to understand and work with complex science and deep technology would be advantageous but is not essential. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.   Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter . To help us understand more about your experience and approach to work, briefly respond to the following questions in your cover letter:   1. Share an example of a venture, company or commercial opportunity you have helped build or grow. What did you contribute? 2. Choose an early-stage opportunity you worked on where the commercial path was not obvious. How did you determine where to focus, and what informed your priorities? 3. Tell us about a time you needed to move an important initiative forward without having direct authority over the people involved. How did you build support and create momentum? You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Want to know more? For queries related to this position, contact Lucy Porter, Executive Recruitment Partner , at lucy.porter@unimelb.edu.au   Apply today and join a community shaping the future of innovation and entrepreneurship.   Applications close: Sunday, 4th October 11:55 PM Melbourne time.   Position Description: PD - JR-017234 Venture Partner, MEC.pdf PD - JR-017234 Venture Partner, MEC.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Clinical-Sites-Metro--Regional/Research-Assistant--Palliative-Medicine_JR-018197": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.5 FTE or 0.6 FTE); Fixed term for 24 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Department of Medicine, St Vincent’s Hospital; Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 plus 17% super Impactful palliative care research role / build mixed-methods and data skills / collaborate across clinical and community settings Apply now to grow your health services research career while improving palliative care for people with advanced illness. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will support high-impact translational and health services research in palliative care across clinical and community settings. You will expand your mixed-methods skills while contributing to better outcomes for people with advanced illness. You will work closely with leading clinicians, researchers, consumers and community partners in the Palliative Nexus group. What you will deliver: Support day-to-day conduct of palliative care research projects, including recruitment, data collection, entry, cleaning and record management. Manage and maintain high-quality research datasets from clinical records, surveys, interviews, registries and patient-reported outcome measures. Undertake descriptive quantitative analysis and contribute to qualitative or mixed-methods analysis under senior researcher guidance. Assist with ethics and governance submissions, reporting, study documentation, secure data storage and compliance with approvals and policies. Contribute to scholarly outputs, presentations and knowledge translation materials for academic, clinical, consumer and community audiences. You may be a great fit if you: Hold a tertiary qualification in health, public health, psychology, social science or related discipline. Demonstrate experience supporting research in health, academic or clinical settings, including data collection and study documentation. Bring strong organisational skills, attention to detail and ability to manage competing tasks and timelines. Contribute to quantitative, qualitative or mixed-methods analysis, reporting, publications, presentations or knowledge translation activities. Use or quickly learn tools such as REDCap, Excel, SPSS, Stata, R, NVivo or similar platforms. Show genuine interest in palliative care, oncology, implementation science, health equity or health services research. For further information, please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team Joining the Department of Medicine at St Vincent’s Hospital, you will be part of a research-active, collaborative environment within Melbourne Medical School. The Palliative Nexus group leads translational and health services research that informs real-world care for people with advanced illness. You will work alongside clinicians, researchers and community partners who value compassion, rigorous methodology and inclusive, patient-centred outcomes, while accessing the resources and support of a leading global university. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload a separate document addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Jennifer Philip via email at jphilip@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid Abdullatif at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 28 September at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-018197 Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine PD.docx JR-018197 Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Academic-Programs-Coordinator--wellbeing-_JR-017700-2": {
      "title": "Academic Programs Coordinator (wellbeing)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed Term until May 2027 Faculty: Melbourne Law School Salary: UoM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Impactful frontline student support / collaboration with academics and University services / wellbeing and health promotion leadership Apply now to coordinate wellbeing, exams and adjustments that directly support Melbourne Law School students. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will deliver responsive academic adjustment and wellbeing support for Melbourne Law School students. You will coordinate exams, extensions and health promotion activities, partnering with academics and University services to enhance outcomes. What you will deliver:   Provide confidential consultations to students on special consideration, adjustments and referrals to internal and external support services. Liaise with University Services, lecturers and ASO colleagues to implement equitable arrangements for students with special requirements. Coordinate administrative processes for special, alternative and supplementary exams, extensions, timetables and assessment-related enquiries. Lead development and continuous improvement of Teaching and Learning web content on wellbeing, mental health and resources. Plan and support wellbeing and health promotion events, including Orientation, Wellness Week and major school activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant tertiary qualification complemented by experience in student support, academic administration or related services. Provide informed advice on academic adjustments, special consideration and outcomes within complex policy and regulatory frameworks. Interpret and apply government legislation, University statutes, policies and procedures with accuracy and sound judgement. Manage competing priorities and deadlines with strong organisational skills and attention to administrative detail. Communicate clearly in writing and verbally, building productive relationships with students, academics and professional staff. Use advanced computer skills across Microsoft Office, email and student management systems to streamline workflows and reporting. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Melbourne Law School You will join the Teaching and Learning team within the Academic Support Office at Melbourne Law School. We are a collaborative, student-centred group, providing high-quality academic and wellbeing support across a diverse cohort. Working with academic leaders and University services, you will help deliver initiatives that strengthen student equity and success. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Cassie Kearns at Cassandra.kearns@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Monday 21 September at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Word: Academic Programs Coordinator - Wellbeing (UoM 6)_PD.docx PDF: Academic Programs Coordinator - Wellbeing (UoM 6)_PD.pdf"
