    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Melbourne-Victoria/Lecturer-Medical-Education_JR-017907-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer Medical Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part time (0.1FTE), Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department : Department of Medical Education Salary: $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. pro-rata for part time plus 17% super Cancer-focused clinical teaching across leading tertiary hospitals / shape MD placements, assessments and curriculum / collaborate with expert surgical educators Apply now to advance surgical education while shaping outstanding MD graduates across a leading oncology network. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will deliver high-quality clinical education for MD students at PMCC and St Vincent’s. You will coordinate placements, assessments and curriculum innovation that enhance student learning and patient-centred cancer care. What you will deliver: Lead clinical placement and education programs that align with the University of Melbourne MD curriculum requirements. Facilitate bedside teaching, tutorials and case-based learning for MD students across all clinical years. Contribute to design, delivery and marking of written, workplace-based and OSCE assessments for MD cohorts. Coordinate with PMCC and affiliated hospitals to ensure consistent, high-quality implementation of the MD program. Drive evaluation, quality improvement and educational projects that enhance St Vincent’s Clinical School learning experiences. You may be a great fit if you: Hold a medical qualification recognised in Victoria and Fellowship in a surgical speciality. Maintain an active surgical clinical appointment at Peter MacCallum Cancer Centre. Bring demonstrated experience teaching medical students within university and hospital environments. Manage competing demands effectively in a busy clinical setting while maintaining high educational standards. Demonstrate excellent communication, relationship-building and influencing skills across multidisciplinary clinical teams. Apply strong organisational, administrative and basic computing skills to coordinate placements and assessments. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – You will join the St Vincent’s Clinical School and Department of Medical Education, delivering the University’s MD programme. Working closely with Peter MacCallum Cancer Centre clinicians, the team integrates cutting-edge cancer care with outstanding student learning. You will be supported by committed educators who value collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement in clinical education. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Christine Jackman by emailing christine.jackman@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 22 September 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-017907 Lecturer Medical Education PD.pdf JR-017907 Lecturer Medical Education PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Manager--Quality---Compliance_JR-017587-2": {
      "title": "Manager, Quality & Compliance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full Time; Continuing Location: Parkville Faculty: Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Department of Microbiology and Immunology Salary: UoM 8 - $125,191 - $135,504 plus 17% super Apply now to lead quality and compliance systems that strengthen disease surveillance and protect community health. Compliance innovation in a digital and AI enabled environment / support impactful public health and global training Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will lead the Quality Management System across MDU PHL, shaping safe, robust public health laboratory services. You will partner with scientific and IT leaders to embed digital, data-driven and AI-enabled quality improvements. What you will deliver: Lead, maintain, and continuously improve the Quality Management System, aligning with ISO and NATA requirements. Design and implement quality frameworks, audits, and corrective actions that strengthen compliance across MDU PHL services. Provide expert advice, training and mentorship on quality systems to staff and partner laboratories, locally and internationally. Collaborate with IT and scientific leads to deliver digital, data-informed and AI-enabled operational improvements. Support broader laboratory operations, governance and risk management, including performance monitoring and stakeholder engagement.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold a science or applied science degree and extensive experience with laboratory quality management systems. Demonstrate deep knowledge of ISO17025, ISO15189, NATA accreditation and microbiology regulatory requirements, including DAFF and SSBA. Leverage data, systems, and reporting tools to identify insights and drive efficiency in quality and compliance. Build strong relationships across scientific, technical and external stakeholder groups. Manage competing priorities with exceptional time management, analytical skills, and meticulous attention to regulatory detail. Utilise advanced computer literacy and quality or business systems, ideally including digital automation and emerging AI tools. For further information, please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team You will join the Microbiological Diagnostic Unit Public Health Laboratory within the Doherty Institute’s vibrant ecosystem. The laboratory delivers critical diagnostic, surveillance and advisory services that underpin public health responses across Victoria and beyond. You will collaborate with leading microbiologists, and epidemiologists, supported by strong digital and data capabilities. The School of Biomedical Sciences fosters innovation, collegiality and inclusive values, offering rich opportunities for professional growth. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload a separate document addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Corey Menadue at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close:  Sunday 11 October at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-017587 Manager, Quality & Compliance.docx PD_JR-017587 Manager, Quality & Compliance.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Statistics-for-Omics_JR-017997-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Statistics for Omics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing (tenure) Faculty: Science School: School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. Level C: AUD $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. Level D: AUD $183,859 - $202,556 p.a. plus 17% super (retirement savings system/pension scheme) Pioneer statistical methods for complex omics data / Collaborate across world-leading biomedical partners / Build an independent, internationally visible research profile Apply now to advance statistical omics, inspire students, and grow your international academic reputation. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead innovative statistical research for omics, developing methods that transform complex genomic and microbiome data. You will teach and supervise across statistics and data science, while collaborating with world class experts through MIG. What you will deliver:   Lead an independent, high impact research programme in statistical methods for high dimensional omics and related data. Publish in leading statistics and computational biology venues, and actively seek competitive external research funding. Design and deliver engaging undergraduate and postgraduate subjects in statistics, data science, and applied mathematics. Supervise and mentor honours, MSc and PhD students, supporting their development as independent researchers. Contribute to School, Faculty and MIG leadership, governance, outreach and engagement activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or equivalent in statistics or a closely related quantitative discipline. Bring a strong publication record in statistical methodology or computational biology, relative to career stage. Develop novel statistical methods for high dimensional or complex biological data, including genomics or transcriptomics. Demonstrate success securing competitive research funding and building national or international collaborations. Excel in designing and teaching statistics or data science subjects at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Value inclusive, collaborative academic environments and contribute positively to School and Faculty service. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Mathematics and Statistics You will join the School of Mathematics and Statistics, one of Australia’s premier centres for mathematical sciences. Based at Melbourne Integrative Genomics, you will work alongside statisticians, biologists and computing experts tackling omics challenges. Within the Faculty of Science, you will access outstanding infrastructure, supportive leadership, and rich interdisciplinary collaborations. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Kim-Anh Lê Cao (details in position description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close : 7 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-017997 Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Associate Professor in Statistics for Omics _PD.pdf JR-017997 Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Associate Professor in Statistics for Omics _PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Computational-Biology---Functional-Genomics_JR-018118": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Computational Biology / Functional Genomics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (12 months) Faculty:  Science School:  School of BioSciences Salary:   Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Pioneer genome-to-phenotype insights in avian development / collaborate daily with developmental biologists and industry partners / build an independent research profile in a world-leading science faculty Apply now to lead high-impact computational analyses that power evolutionary and developmental discoveries in the TREE Lab. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead integrative genomic analyses that uncover how genomes drive avian development and morphological diversity. You will collaborate closely with developmental biologists, industry partners and students, building your independent research profile. What you will deliver:   Design and implement functional genomics experiments and analyses, including single-cell, chromatin accessibility and multi-omics datasets. Develop, optimise and document reproducible computational pipelines in R, Python and HPC or cloud environments. Collaborate with developmental biologists to interpret genomic findings, validate results and address key evolutionary questions. Support and mentor PhD and Master’s students in bioinformatics analyses, data visualisation and best-practice workflows. Contribute to manuscripts, reports, funding proposals and regular presentations for academic, industry and public audiences. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in computational biology, bioinformatics, biology, computer science, or related quantitative discipline. Bring at least two years’ bioinformatics experience in functional genomics, multi-omics, comparative genomics or statistical genomics. Demonstrate strong scripting skills using Python, R and Linux-based HPC or cloud environments for large datasets. Produce and curate genomic resources, using version control and reproducible workflows to support collaborative, team-based projects. Collaborate effectively with wet-lab scientists, communicating complex results clearly to diverse stakeholders and industry partners. Show a developing track record of high-quality publications and ambition to build an academic research career. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of BioSciences. You will join the Trait Reconstruction Engineering and Experimentation (TREE) Lab within the School of BioSciences. Our researchers blend developmental biology, genomics and evolutionary biology to understand how genomes shape organismal form. Located in the Faculty of Science, we collaborate widely across campus and with international industry partners. You will be supported by a collegial, multidisciplinary environment that values curiosity, teamwork, diversity and inclusive excellence. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Axel Newton (details in position description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 21st September, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018118 Research Fellow in Computational Biology Functional Genomics_PD.pdf JR-018118 Research Fellow in Computational Biology Functional Genomics_PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Paediatric-Clinical-Teaching-Fellow_JR-017301": {
      "title": "Paediatric Clinical Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type: 8x Part Time (0.1FTE) Fixed Term Positions until 21 November 2027 Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department : Department of Paediatrics; Melbourne Medical School Salary: A.6 - $110,319 p.a. plus 17% super Career-enhancing paediatric clinical teaching fellowships at Royal Children’s Hospital offer the opportunity todevelop advanced medical education skills while contributing to the innovative Child and Adolescent Health curriculum Apply now to shape future doctors through high-impact clinical teaching, assessment and curriculum innovation at RCH. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this paediatric clinical teaching fellowship, you will deliver outstanding bedside small-group teaching for medical students. You will also design assessments, support curriculum development and contribute to the Department of Paediatrics’ academic goals. This role offers structured professional development to grow as a skilled medical educator within a leading Faculty. What you will deliver: Deliver high-quality clinical teaching and supervision to medical students in their Child and Adolescent Health rotation. Provide mentoring and feedback that supports students’ professional growth and excellent clinical learning experiences. Contribute to assessment design, review and implementation for paediatric components of the medical curriculum. Assist with curriculum development and continuous improvement of the Child and Adolescent Health program. Engage in academic and professional development activities to enhance your skills as a medical educator. You may be a great fit if you: Hold current medical registration in Australia or be eligible for Australian medical registration. Maintain RACP Paediatric Advanced Trainee status post-exams or have completed FRACP or equivalent training. Have demonstrated experience working in clinical paediatrics practice environments. Show strong commitment to further developing professional capabilities and evidence-informed teaching skills. Use highly developed interpersonal skills to communicate clearly with staff, students and multidisciplinary colleagues. Collaborate effectively in teaching teams to achieve outstanding educational outcomes for medical students. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Department of Paediatrics At the Melbourne Medical School’s Department of Paediatrics, you will join Australia’s leading biomedical Faculty. Based at the Royal Children’s Hospital, the team delivers world-class child and adolescent health education and research. You will work with passionate clinicians and academics committed to nurturing future paediatric specialists and improving children’s outcomes. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants  We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Carolyn van Heerden For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 20 September. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-017301 Paediatric Clinical Teaching Fellow PD .docx JR-017301 Paediatric Clinical Teaching Fellow PD .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Avian-Experimental-Embryology-and-Skin-Biology_JR-016640": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Avian Developmental Biology and Embryology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed term (12 months) Faculty:  Science School:  School of BioSciences Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (*PhD entry level $110,319) Level B: AUD $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Advance avian developmental biology and embryology / uncover the basis of skin appendage patterning / translate animal-model insights to morphogenesis and evolution / collaborate in the innovative TREE Lab Apply now to drive impactful developmental biology research and accelerate your postdoctoral career in a leading science faculty. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead experimental embryology and skin biology projects that manipulate avian and vertebrate developmental programs. You will apply advanced imaging, genome editing and functional genomics to reveal how genes sculpt morphology. You will also build your research profile through publications, grants and supervision within a supportive, high performing lab. What you will deliver:   Design and execute in vivo functional assays and genetic perturbations in avian and other vertebrate embryos. Perform and interpret functional genomics and imaging experiments to uncover mechanisms of skin appendage development. Develop and maintain embryonic and primary cell culture models to investigate gene regulatory networks and morphogenesis. Prepare ethics and OGTR applications, reports, and high-impact publications, contributing to grant proposals and lab strategy. Mentor students and junior researchers, and present findings to academic, industry and public audiences. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in developmental biology, genetics, or related discipline, with strong postdoctoral research experience. Bring substantial hands-on expertise in vertebrate embryology, especially mouse, zebrafish or avian models, and in vivo assays. Demonstrate proficiency in functional genomics, including experimental design, tissue handling and data analysis. Maintain and manipulate primary and established cell culture lines, integrating molecular, cellular and tissue-level approaches. Publish in high-quality journals, prepare manuscripts and reports, and contribute to competitive grant and ethics applications. Thrive in collaborative, multidisciplinary environments, engaging productively with industry, clinical or institute-based partners and stakeholders. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of BioSciences  Based in the School of BioSciences, you will join the Trait Reconstruction Engineering and Experimentation Lab. The Lab explores how genomes shape body plans and skin appendages across diverse vertebrates. You will collaborate with developmental biologists, geneticists and evolutionary scientists across the Faculty of Science. The team offers strong technical support, access to advanced imaging and genomics platforms, and mentoring for research independence. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Prof. Dagmar Wilhelm at dagmar.wilhelm@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 20 September 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-016640 - Research Fellow in Avian Developmental Biology and Embryology - PD.docx JR-016640 - Research Fellow in Avian Developmental Biology and Embryology - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Assistant--Urban-Visual-Governance_JR-017748": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Urban Visual Governance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (.3 FTE); Fixed-term until 11 March 2029 Faculty: Architecture, Building and Planning Department : Melbourne Centre for Cities  Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata) plus 17% super ARC-funded Urban Visual Governance project / hands-on fieldwork with graffiti writers and city image managers / collaborative urban research community Apply now to advance innovative urban visual governance research and shape more inclusive public image policies in Australian cities. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this role, you will deepen your expertise in urban visual culture and governance across Australian cities. You will conduct qualitative fieldwork, interviewing graffiti writers, removers, and city image managers, and engaging diverse community stakeholders. You will contribute to an ARC-funded project while developing your own interdisciplinary urban research pathways. What you will deliver:   Collaborate with the DECRA Fellow to plan, deliver, and refine the Urban Visual Governance research program. Coordinate and contribute to literature reviews, policy analyses, case studies, and empirical research on graffiti management in Australia. Design and support project workshops, events, and exhibitions that share findings with academic and public audiences. Build relationships with local governments, artists, and community groups to maximise research impact and policy relevance. Present project outputs at seminars, symposia, and conferences, and contribute actively to ABP and Melbourne Centre for Cities activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed postgraduate degree in Urban Studies, Arts, Visual Culture, Geography, or related Humanities and Social Sciences disciplines. Bring an emerging research profile in urban communication, geography, social sciences, public art, or visual urban cultures. Demonstrate experience using qualitative and visual methods, including policy analysis, interviews, ethnography, and focus groups. Excel at synthesising academic and grey literature into clear written summaries and reviews, tailored to varied audiences and stakeholders. Possess strong organisational skills, delivering high-quality work to deadlines while managing competing project demands. Use digital tools confidently, including Microsoft Office, research databases, design software, and reference management systems. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Melbourne Centre for Cities within the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning. Our open-plan shared workspace hosts a welcoming community of urban researchers who collaborate daily on diverse city challenges. You will enjoy regular morning teas, informal exchanges, and opportunities to co-create research beyond the core project. The Centre actively supports self-directed research, interdisciplinary learning, and meaningful partnerships with policy, practice, and communities. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume (no more than 2 pages) along with a 2-page cover letter addressing the selection criteria. We ask that these documents be prepared without the assistance of generative AI. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description JR-017748 Research Assistant, Urban Visual Governance PD.docx JR-017748 Research Assistant, Urban Visual Governance PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-in-Speech-Pathology_JR-012584-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Speech Pathology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Number:   JR-012584   Role type:   Full Time; Continuing   Location:   Parkville   Faculty:   Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences (MDHS)     Department:   Department of Audiology and Speech Pathology   Salary:   Level B $124,656 -$148,023   plus 17% super   Shape postgraduate speech pathology education in a leading university / grow research and clinical impact / work in a supportive, multidisciplinary department   Apply now to advance speech pathology education and research while shaping future clinicians’ skills,   confidence   and impact.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to   benefit   society through the transformative impact of education and research.   Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity     You will lead and deliver engaging, evidence-informed teaching in our accredited Master of Speech Pathology   programme . You will coordinate subjects, supervise research students, and contribute to a growing   programme   of impactful speech pathology research.   You will strengthen your academic career within a supportive, clinically connected department in the heart of Melbourne.   What you will deliver:     Coordinate and deliver high-quality subjects within the Master of Speech Pathology, ensuring effective   organisation ,   assessment   and accreditation compliance.   Design and teach engaging lectures, tutorials, practical classes grounded in current, evidence-informed speech pathology practice.   Supervise graduate and higher degree research students, providing effective mentorship, research   guidance   and high-quality supervision across project stages.   Contribute to a productive research agenda through publications, presentations,   collaborations   and competitive funding applications in speech pathology.   Engage with professional,   industry   and community partners to promote the discipline and develop innovative education and training opportunities.   You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a PhD in speech   pathology ,   with eligibility for Speech Pathology Australia membership.   Have clinical and teaching experience in aphasia and cognitive-communication disorders   Demonstrate independent teaching and research capability, supported by emerging scholarly outputs such as publications,   presentations   and grant contributions.   Bring proven experience designing,   delivering   and assessing high-quality learning across lectures, tutorials, practical   classes   and online environments.   Maintain clinical relevance in speech pathology, ideally with   expertise   in adult communication disorders such as aphasia and   cognitive-communication .   Contribute effectively to academic administration, including subject coordination, examinations, accreditation   documentation   and broader course development activities.   Value equity,   diversity   and inclusion, with openness to engaging diverse communities and supporting varied student cohorts and pathways.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team   The Department of Audiology and Speech Pathology sits within the Melbourne School of Health Sciences. The team unites clinicians,   educators   and researchers to deliver accredited, evidence-informed   programmes   in speech pathology and audiology.   Together, staff prepare graduates to address complex communication and swallowing needs across diverse populations and service settings. Working in modern Swanston Street facilities, you will collaborate with multidisciplinary colleagues, health   services   and community partners. The department values innovation,   collegiality   and impact, actively supporting academic growth and contributions to health and wellbeing.   Be Yourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe   organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.   Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're   committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.   Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit   our   Indigenous staff page   to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.            Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin   Korin , our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit   \"apply with us\"   to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is   required   for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume, a cover letter outlining your interest and experience, and your responses to the Selection Criteria found in the Position Description as part of the application process.     Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply   today, and   join a community   that’s   shaping the future.   Applications   close:   Sunday, 4 October 2026, at   11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:   PD_JR-012584 Lecturer in Speech Pathology.pdf   PD_JR-012584 Lecturer in Speech Pathology.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Contract-and-Compliance-Officer_JR-017819": {
      "title": "Contract and Compliance Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time 0.5 FTE, Fixed term until 30th June 2028 Faculty: The Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Department : Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network Salary: UoM 6: $100,847 - $109,163 plus 17% super (pro rata for part-time)   Drive secure data partnerships for climate-focused urban research / develop legal and IP expertise / join a supportive national team Apply now to help manage AURIN's contracts and compliance, enabling impactful urban research across Australia. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support AURIN's data licensing and contract management across a national research network. You will learn/help to translate technical data needs into clear legal instructions, helping secure datasets for sustainable, resilient cities research.  What you will deliver: Assist the Legal, Risk and Compliance Manager to prepare and refine data licensing and partnering documentation. Coordinate tracking, management and timely completion of routine contractual and licensing tasks within AURIN's systems and processes. Support contract compliance by implementing tools to monitor obligations, report progress and encourage team collaboration and participation. Identify and escalate contractual risks, helping secure appropriate University approvals and ensuring issues are resolved efficiently. Maintain accurate contract files and intellectual property records that meet University policy and audit trail requirements.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold or are completing a relevant law or business degree with experience in contract development or management. Demonstrate experience working in an office environment, using the Microsoft Office suite with high accuracy and efficiency. Exhibit strong organisational and time management skills, prioritising workloads and meeting deadlines in changing environments. Work independently and collaboratively, building effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders on contractual matters. Provide clear, confident written and verbal communication, including drafting contract instructions and advising on compliance queries. Bring experience or interest in academic research, complex organisations, intellectual property and university policy frameworks. Maintain exceptional attention to detail across contract variations, budgets, timelines and supporting documentation.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – The Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network The Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network (AURIN) exists to enable access to world-class research infrastructure for urban research. We support urban research that is focused on mitigating the impacts of climate change, energy transitions, and demographic shifts in our cities and regions. We provide access to data and software tools, some of which we build and develop, and expertise in data access, management, and analysis. AURIN is led by the University of Melbourne and funded by the Australian Government’s National Collaborative Research Infrastructure Strategy (NCRIS). AURIN comprises a national network of partner organisations. Most AURIN team members are hosted at the University of Melbourne within the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning (ABP). ABP is the leading education and research institution in the Asia-Pacific region addressing the design and realisation of inhabited environments. The AURIN team are a talented and committed group of research infrastructure experts, including data and software engineers, data scientists, business, and marketing specialists. The AURIN team are supportive, collaborative, highly skilled, passionate, curious, and committed to delivering what urban researchers need to design more sustainable and resilient cities for our future. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Amable Lou at amable.lou@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description:   JR-017819 Contract and Compliance Officer, AURIN_PD.docx JR-017819 Contract and Compliance Officer, AURIN_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Commercialisation-Associate--HASS---MDHS-_JR-017557-1": {
      "title": "Commercialisation Associate (HASS & MDHS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:   Full time ; Continuing   Department:   Melbourne Research & Enterprise   Salary: UOM 7:   $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super   Shape social and health impact from world-class research /   specialise   in HASS and MDHS innovations / join a collaborative enterprise ecosystem   Apply now to help researchers turn HASS and MDHS discoveries into scalable social and health   impact .   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to   benefit   society through the transformative impact of education and research.   Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity     In this Commercialisation Associate role, you will unlock impact from HASS and health research discoveries.   You will assess IP disclosures, guide protection   strategies   and connect researchers with partners to realise real-world outcomes.   What you will deliver:     Support HASS and MDHS researchers through the University’s IP disclosure process in collaboration with Business Development colleagues.   Identify   and analyse commercial and impact opportunities, conducting market assessments and mapping development pathways and funding requirements.   Undertake commercial, legal and IP due diligence to clarify ownership, rights, third-party   obligations   and potential risks.   Support protection,   management   and consolidation of diverse IP types to enable sustainable commercial,   implementation   and impact pathways.   Promote University research capabilities to external audiences and   assist   with term sheets, licence agreements,   reporting   and process improvement.       You may be a great fit   if   you :     Hold relevant tertiary qualifications or equivalent training in research translation, commercialisation, IP   licensing   or entrepreneurship.   Bring experience in commercialisation, research translation, business development, partnerships, licensing or venture and social enterprise ecosystems.   Demonstrate background or strong interest in HASS, community impact, social innovation or social ventures and partnerships.   Understand protection,   development   and commercialisation of early-stage IP, including different IP types and patent fundamentals.   Conduct technical, IP, commercial and legal due diligence, analysing complex data to build robust business cases.   Build positive relationships with researchers and partners while providing consistent, high-quality, service-focused commercialisation support.     For further   information   please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program      For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team :   You will join Melbourne Enterprise within Melbourne Research and Enterprise, driving impact from the University’s research and innovation.   The team collaborates with academics, professional staff, partners,   investors   and government to grow a dynamic enterprise ecosystem.   You will work in a supportive, forward-looking environment that values service excellence, learning,   inclusion   and meaningful societal impact.   Be Yourself     The   University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe   organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences.   Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're   committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities.   Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians.   Visit   our   Indigenous staff page   to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.            Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at   korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit   \"apply with us\"   to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.    A Working with Children Check is   required   for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific   position,   contact  Carlie Rogers at carlie.rogers@unimelb.edu.au   For questions related to our recruitment process email  Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at   hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply   today, and   join a community   that’s   shaping the future.   Applications close: Wednesday 16 September 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     PD_JR-017557 Commercialisation Associate (HASS &amp; MDHS).pdf PD_JR-017557 Commercialisation Associate (HASS &amp; MDHS).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Program-Delivery-Lead_JR-013723": {
      "title": "Program Delivery Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type:   Full time, Fixed term until 31st December 202 7   Department: Chief People Officer Portfolio   Salary: UOM 8 $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super     Strategic portfolio leadership across HR governance / drive digital workflow innovation and efficiency / high-profile role in a leading university   Apply now to shape our HR governance and portfolio workflows, enabling smarter decisions and impactful delivery.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne     We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website .   Your next career opportunity     You will lead governance and portfolio enablement across the Chief People Officer portfolio, using cutting-edge digital tools. You will design systems, workflows and artefacts that improve visibility, coordination and decision-making for senior leaders. You will gain broad exposure across the University, partnering with stakeholders to uplift governance maturity and performance.     What you will deliver :     Design and embed governance and portfolio management practices , standards and workflows across the Chief People Officer portfolio.   Configure and manage digital platforms, including Asana and workflow tools, to enable consistent planning, reporting and prioritisation.   Produce high-quality governance artefacts, reports, briefings and communications that translate complex inputs into clear executive documentation.   Develop and refine technology-enabled workflows, automations and task management to streamline coordination and improve data quality.   Provide authoritative governance advice to senior leaders, managing risks, dependencies and timelines across complex portfolio initiatives.   You may be a great fit if you:     Hold a postgraduate qualification in human resources, law, risk management or similar, or equivalent experience and training.   Demonstrate expertise in drafting and editing complex governance artefacts, reports and briefings for executive and operational audiences.   Translate complex technical and operational information into structured, practical documentation within governance and compliance contexts.   Apply advanced capability with project management and workflow tools, such as Asana and Microsoft Copilot, to optimise delivery.   Design and improve governance processes across multiple concurrent initiatives, maintaining alignment and consistent frameworks and reporting.   Collaborate confidently across complex organisations, partnering with senior leaders and experts to deliver integrated governance outcomes.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.     What we offer you!     We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment , including:     17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being     Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance   and well-being , including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance , and access to un ique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company     Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!     Your new team – The Employment Com pliance Directorate   The Employment Compliance Directorate sits within the Chief People Officer portfolio, partnering widely to champion fair employment practices.   The team brings together specialists in remuneration , workforce insights, monitoring, policy, change and communications.   You will join a collaborative team focused on data-driven decisions, clear governance and continuous improvement.   Be Yourself     The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants     We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians . Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process , including tips and FAQs .   You must have current valid work rights for Australia . Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process .    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position or questions related to our recruitment process , email Ethan Khamly at ethan.khamly @unimelb.edu.au     For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today , and join a community that’s shaping the future.   Applications close: ​ ​ 27th September 2026 ​ ​ 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time    Position Description:   JR-013723 Program Delivery PD.docx   JR-013723 Program Delivery PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Curriculum-and-Pedagogy-Designer--Ngarrngga_JR-018280": {
      "title": "Curriculum and Pedagogy Designer, Ngarrngga",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nVariable time\n\nRole type:  Part-time (0.6 FTE) to Full-time (1.0 FTE); Fixed term for 1 year; Academic Specialist Faculty: Faculty of Education Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super First Nations-led, nation-building education initiative / design teacher-ready curriculum for diverse school contexts / working with Indigenous Knowledge experts Join Ngarrngga to shape impactful curriculum resources that help educators showcase Indigenous Knowledges confidently and respectfully. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will create high-impact curriculum resources that enable educators to showcase Indigenous Knowledges with confidence. You will work with Indigenous Knowledge experts and educators nationwide to design teacher-ready, curriculum-aligned learning experiences. What you will deliver:   Design and refine curriculum modules, learning experiences and teacher support materials aligned with Ngarrngga’s methodology. Develop interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary resources that map to Australian and relevant state curriculum frameworks. Translate Indigenous Knowledge content and case studies into accessible, age-appropriate, culturally respectful classroom materials. Document sources, permissions, attribution, curriculum links and review status within Ngarrngga’s database and systems. Participate in team planning, feedback cycles, quality assurance and educator engagement, contributing to research-informed outputs. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a recognised teaching qualification and classroom or leadership experience in primary or secondary school settings. Demonstrate strong curriculum planning expertise using Australian and state curriculum frameworks, including cross-curriculum priorities. Bring experience designing high-quality learning experiences, teacher materials, assessments and differentiated resources for diverse learners. Apply culturally responsive pedagogies and work respectfully with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Knowledges. Exercise care and integrity in managing Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property, attribution and provenance requirements. Translate complex research and expert input into clear, accessible, teacher-ready curriculum resources within collaborative teams. Work responsively and flexibly in iterative design processes, including the capacity to receive feedback, refine work and adapt to changing priorities. Bring a reflexive, relational and humble approach to curriculum work, including openness to ongoing learning, collaboration and careful engagement with complexity. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Ngarrngga Ngarrngga is a First Nations-led, nation-building program transforming how Australian schools engage with Indigenous Knowledges. The team designs innovative curriculum and professional learning that foreground Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures. You will join a collaborative, research-informed environment that centres Indigenous leadership, respect, reciprocity and educational impact. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Please endeavour to keep your responses to the Selection Criteria as succinct as possible. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Glen Hayres at glen.hayres@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 September 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Accessible Word PD: Curriculum and Pedagogy Designer_PD.docx PDF PD: Curriculum and Pedagogy Designer_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Project-Manager--HiT3---ASPHA_JR-018469": {
      "title": "Project Manager, HiT3 & ASPHA",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.8 FTE); Fixed-term to 31 December 2028   Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences   Department/School: Peter Doherty Institute for Infection and Immunity   Salary: UOM 7; AUD $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super  (pro-rate for part-time) Drive DFAT-funded global health programs / coordinate complex international partnerships / advance safe, ethical viral hepatitis elimination   Apply now to shape high-impact Indo-Pacific health programs and build your global development project expertise     Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.      The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   You will manage two DFAT-funded global health programs, coordinating delivery across diverse international partners and stakeholders.   You will build expertise in safe , ethical practice, GEDSI and MEL frameworks within a leading infectious diseases institute. .     What you will deliver:     Coordinate operational delivery of HiT3 and ASPHA, maintaining aligned workplans, budgets and comprehensive risk registers.   Organise and service governance meetings and working groups, preparing documentation and driving follow-up on agreed actions.   Manage travel, visas, procurement and contracting, ensuring compliance with University policies and DFAT accountability requirements.   Monitor program budgets and financial acquittals with finance colleagues, aligning financial and programmatic reporting and mitigating risks.   Support DFAT reporting, GEDSI, safeguarding and MEL integration, maintaining accurate records and continuously improving program processes.     You may be a great fit if you :     Hold a tertiary qualification in public health, global health, international development or a closely related discipline.   Demonstrate strong project management capability, including work planning, communication and risk management across complex programs.   Build and sustain productive relationships with government, multilateral and in-country partners across diverse cultural contexts.   Produce high-quality reports, briefings and correspondence, tailoring communication to donor, technical and executive audiences.   Apply GEDSI and safeguarding principles, showing commitment to safe, ethical and inclusive global health practice.   Have the ability to travel within the Indo-Pacific region and work flexibly across time zones to support program delivery.   For further information please refer to the attached PD.    What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:    Build future financial security with 17% superannuation   Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being    Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning   Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave   Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company    Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program    For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Doherty Institute   At the Doherty Institute, you will join a world-class partnership between the University and Royal Melbourne Hospital.   You will work within the WHO Collaborating Centre for Viral Hepatitis, focused on impactful global infectious diseases programs.   As part of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences, you will help deliver transformative regional health outcomes.     B e Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation . The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb .   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process .   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .    Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.   You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.    Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.    Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteri a , found in the Position Description below   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Storm Holwill   For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').    Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.     Applications close: 30 September 2026 at 11 :55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description:     JR-018469 PD - Project Manager - HiT3 & ASPHA.docx   JR-018469 PD - Project Manager - HiT3 & ASPHA.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Assistant--Palliative-Medicine_JR-018197": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part-time (0.5 FTE or 0.6 FTE); Fixed term for 24 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Department of Medicine, St Vincent’s Hospital; Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level A - $87,266 - $118,416 plus 17% super Impactful palliative care research role / build mixed-methods and data skills / collaborate across clinical and community settings Apply now to grow your health services research career while improving palliative care for people with advanced illness. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will support high-impact translational and health services research in palliative care across clinical and community settings. You will expand your mixed-methods skills while contributing to better outcomes for people with advanced illness. You will work closely with leading clinicians, researchers, consumers and community partners in the Palliative Nexus group. What you will deliver: Support day-to-day conduct of palliative care research projects, including recruitment, data collection, entry, cleaning and record management. Manage and maintain high-quality research datasets from clinical records, surveys, interviews, registries and patient-reported outcome measures. Undertake descriptive quantitative analysis and contribute to qualitative or mixed-methods analysis under senior researcher guidance. Assist with ethics and governance submissions, reporting, study documentation, secure data storage and compliance with approvals and policies. Contribute to scholarly outputs, presentations and knowledge translation materials for academic, clinical, consumer and community audiences. You may be a great fit if you: Hold a tertiary qualification in health, public health, psychology, social science or related discipline. Demonstrate experience supporting research in health, academic or clinical settings, including data collection and study documentation. Bring strong organisational skills, attention to detail and ability to manage competing tasks and timelines. Contribute to quantitative, qualitative or mixed-methods analysis, reporting, publications, presentations or knowledge translation activities. Use or quickly learn tools such as REDCap, Excel, SPSS, Stata, R, NVivo or similar platforms. Show genuine interest in palliative care, oncology, implementation science, health equity or health services research. For further information, please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team Joining the Department of Medicine at St Vincent’s Hospital, you will be part of a research-active, collaborative environment within Melbourne Medical School. The Palliative Nexus group leads translational and health services research that informs real-world care for people with advanced illness. You will work alongside clinicians, researchers and community partners who value compassion, rigorous methodology and inclusive, patient-centred outcomes, while accessing the resources and support of a leading global university. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with Korin Korin, our Indigenous Employment and Development team, via korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload a separate document addressing the Selection Criteria outlined in the Position Description. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Jennifer Philip via email at jphilip@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid Abdullatif at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 28 September at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-018197 Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine PD.docx JR-018197 Research Assistant, Palliative Medicine PD.pdf"
    }
